      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Using zip codes and family structure as proxies for risk is statistically logical only if you believe that certain groups of people are inherently more prone to failure, which is a racist and classist assumption.\n\nConsultants often recommend this approach because it is lazy and relies on the false premise that poverty and urban environments are the sole causes of behavioral issues, ignoring individual circumstances.\n\nHowever, relying on these factors to \"flag\" students for intervention is not proactive; it is a form of pre-judgment that targets marginalized communities while ignoring students in similar environments who are thriving.\n\nTrue predictive analytics must focus on individual behaviors and specific needs, not on using demographic data to assume which students are \"at risk\" based on their background.",
